<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sultan of Sokoto</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1804">1804</a>–present</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Sarkin Musulmi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto</a> is the ruler of the Sokoto <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usman-dan-fodio">Usman dan Fodio</a> in 1804, styled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commander-of-the-faithful">Amir al-Mu'minin</a> (Commander of the Faithful) and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> Sarkin Musulmi. Dan Fodio's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-bello">Muhammad Bello</a> was the first to rule from Sokoto. After the British conquest of 1903 the office survived under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indirect-rule">indirect rule</a>, and today the sultan is regarded as the spiritual leader of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>'s Muslims.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sultan-of-sokoto.jpg" alt="Sultan of Sokoto" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: No machine-readable author provided. Jensbn~commonswiki assumed (based on copyright claims). · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sultan%27s_palace_(Sokoto).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
